{"causes": ["The intellectual ferment of the Enlightenment, particularly the writings of Voltaire and Rousseau, deeply influenced Lafayette's early commitment to liberty and human rights.", "France's geopolitical rivalry with Great Britain provided a strategic impetus for young Lafayette to volunteer his military services to the American independence movement in 1777.", "His aristocratic upbringing and military training afforded him the social standing and martial skills necessary to pursue a command in the Continental Army at a young age."], "effects": ["Lafayette's leadership at the Siege of Yorktown in 1781 significantly contributed to the American victory and the ultimate securing of American independence.", "His advocacy and drafting efforts for the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen profoundly shaped the early, liberal phase of the French Revolution in 1789.", "Lafayette's career cemented his status as an enduring symbol of transatlantic republicanism and the historical Franco-American alliance, influencing diplomatic and political thought for centuries."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "gilbert-du-motier-marquis-de-lafayette", "sourceName": "Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de Lafayette", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "george-washington", "targetName": "George Washington", "context": "During the American Revolutionary War, Lafayette served as a trusted aide and commander under General Washington from 1777 to 1781."}, {"sourceSlug": "american-revolutionary-war", "sourceName": "American Revolutionary War", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "gilbert-du-motier-marquis-de-lafayette", "targetName": "Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de Lafayette", "context": "Lafayette's service as a major general in the Continental Army from 1777 to 1781 was a defining period of the American Revolutionary War."}, {"sourceSlug": "gilbert-du-motier-marquis-de-lafayette", "sourceName": "Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de Lafayette", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "siege-of-yorktown", "targetName": "Siege of Yorktown", "context": "Lafayette commanded Continental Army troops during the decisive Siege of Yorktown in October 1781, contributing to the British surrender."}, {"sourceSlug": "enlightenment", "sourceName": "Enlightenment", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "gilbert-du-motier-marquis-de-lafayette", "targetName": "Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de Lafayette", "context": "The ideals of liberty and popular sovereignty championed by Enlightenment thinkers deeply influenced Lafayette's political philosophy and actions throughout his life."}, {"sourceSlug": "gilbert-du-motier-marquis-de-lafayette", "sourceName": "Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de Lafayette", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "paris-france", "targetName": "Paris, France", "context": "Lafayette was a prominent political figure in Paris during the French Revolution, commanding the National Guard and advocating for constitutional reform."}], "places": [{"name": "Yorktown, United States", "role": "Major battle site"}, {"name": "Paris, France", "role": "Political center"}, {"name": "Chavaniac-Lafayette, France", "role": "Birthplace"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de Lafayette", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "gilbert-du-motier-marquis-de-lafayette"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q186652", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marquis_de_Lafayette"], "tags": ["France", "Military Leaders & Commanders", "Europe", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "empire-and-colonialism"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 8, "significanceNarrative": "Lafayette's pivotal military contributions to the American Revolution secured independence for the United States, while his early leadership in the French Revolution shaped its initial liberal phase. His enduring legacy as a "Hero of Two Worlds" profoundly influenced transatlantic republican ideals and diplomatic relations for centuries.", "significanceCategory": "continental"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-21T17:38:42Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27912224997", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Marie-Joseph Paul Yves Roch Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de La Fayette, known in the United States as Lafayette, was a French military officer and politician who volunteered to join the Continental Army, led by General George Washington, in the American Revolutionary War. Lafayette commanded Continental Army troops in the decisive siege of Yorktown in 1781, the Revolutionary War's final major battle, which secured American independence. After returning to France, Lafayette became a key figure in the French Revolution of 1789 and the July Revolution of 1830 and continues to be celebrated as a hero in both France and the United States.", "newValue": "Marie-Joseph Paul Yves Roch Gilbert du Motier, Marquis de Lafayette, emerged as a pivotal figure in the late 18th and early 19th centuries, bridging the revolutionary ideals of America and France. Born into French nobility in 1757, Lafayette, driven by Enlightenment principles and a desire to aid the American cause against Great Britain, became a major general in the Continental Army at just 19 years old. His unwavering commitment to liberty and constitutionalism positioned him as a transatlantic symbol of republicanism and a "Hero of Two Worlds," influencing political thought across continents.

Lafayette volunteered for the Continental Army in 1777, quickly earning the trust of General George Washington and distinguishing himself at battles like Brandywine and the winter encampment at Valley Forge. He commanded American troops with skill and courage, playing a crucial role in the decisive Siege of Yorktown in October 1781, which secured American independence. Upon his return to France, Lafayette became a leading voice for reform, serving in the Assembly of Notables and the Estates-General. He presented an early draft of the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen in 1789 and commanded the National Guard during the initial phase of the French Revolution.

Despite his early revolutionary zeal, Lafayette's commitment to constitutional monarchy led to his imprisonment during the radicalization of the French Revolution. Released in 1797, he remained a steadfast liberal, returning to public life under the Bourbon Restoration and advocating for democratic reforms. His involvement in the July Revolution of 1830, which installed Louis-Philippe as a constitutional monarch, solidified his enduring legacy as a champion of liberty and national self-determination, inspiring subsequent generations of revolutionaries and reformers."}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-21T17:38:42Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27912224997", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Early Modern era: age of exploration, printing revolution, and religious reformation", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1757-09-06, Chavaniac-Lafayette"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "1757-09-06, Chavaniac-Lafayette"}], "newValue": ["The intellectual ferment of the Enlightenment, particularly the writings of Voltaire and Rousseau, deeply influenced Lafayette's early commitment to liberty and human rights.", "France's geopolitical rivalry with Great Britain provided a strategic impetus for young Lafayette to volunteer his military services to the American independence movement in 1777.", "His aristocratic upbringing and military training afforded him the social standing and martial skills necessary to pursue a command in the Continental Army at a young age."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-21T17:38:42Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27912224997", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "1834-05-20"}, {"title": "Contributed to global exchange networks, modern state formation, and scientific revolution", "type": "Movement", "year": "1834-05-20"}], "newValue": ["Lafayette's leadership at the Siege of York